this game is a total conversion for Unreal Tournament 2004.

It starts with the mechanics of Warcraft Rumble in the engine of UT2004 with the
assets of UT2004.

The player will have a top-down-ish view (movable via joystick or keyboard)

a special rendering engine will be made to allow for the default or user made
maps to be used. This rendering system will clip or occlude parts of the map
that are between the player and the units being played. It will do this by
raycasting from a unit's position toward the camera, and in a cone that fully
detects the doodads and map geometry sections that would occlude the unit.

It does this dynamically as the player moves the camera. In-fact, it's probably
better to do it from the AI waypoints instead - but if a player is on the ground
playing with the other bots (not in the MVP) then it can detect from each human
player as well as the AI waypoints. For now, it just detects geometry that
occludes the AI waypoints and hides them from view.

Note, each player with the top-down-ish-adjustable view will need a different
set of occlusion, because they will have different perspectives.

---

In terms of mechanics, how it works is each second or so the player will receive
1 adrenaline. When they reach 100 they can't gain any more. They will have,
from their skybox perspective, several guns in their inventory. There will be
several "nodes" (onslaught map) or spawn locations (team deathmatch style) that
they can spawn units at. In the team deathmatch style, they can spawn units on
an AI waypoint that is close to allies but not foes. They will appear to the
player as green floating orbs, while the unavailable ones will be red, and the
unclaimed ones will be gray.

They can spawn a unit at the node they are looking at (which is highlighted as
if selected) by left clicking. This creates an AI bot that behaves according to
the nature of the gun the player has selected. For example, a sniper rifle like
the lightning gun will stay at a distance and fire at their target, ignoring
other enemies. They will select the farthest target they have line of sight on.
But a flak cannon will move close to enemies and attempt to mulch them while
moving left and right to try and dodge incoming projectiles.

Depending on the map mode, the game will end when objectives are complete.

Spawning a unit uses 10 adrenaline. If a unit picks up adrenaline in the map, it
goes to the player. The player has 200 health. If they lose all their health,
they die. They gain health whenever an ally picks up health packs and such
(5 health per 25 healthpack, 1 health per health vial) and they lose health when
one of their units dies (10 health per unit)

If a unit gains an armor powerup, it guards the unit but does not transfer to
the player. The healthpacks heal the units in addition to the player. The AI
bots will not use adrenaline of their own. If they pick up adrenaline powerups,
it goes to the player at a rate of 1 adrenaline per pill pickup.

When the player spawns a unit, it discards the gun (removing it from the game)
that they have equipped and spawns the unit with it. Then, they receive another
random gun that isn't the one they just spawned. It's a weighted random, meaning
they have to cycle through all the guns before they can get the first one again.

the player will have 4 guns at any one time.

the game will be a total conversion mod built for the Linux version. It should
not use features that were added in later versions of the game, but a separate
version should be made for the most common or most up-to-date version of the
game that is functionally identical. This is for compatibility reasons - the
real version is the Linux version, and development should treat that as the
target.

---

the AI bots should spread out through the level and attempt to fulfil objectives
if they are nearby. They will choose which route to take through the level
randomly, but they will avoid paths they have taken already, resetting on death.
In this way, they will naturally spread out through the map and avoid loopbacks
and getting stuck.


